body:
I discovered a bug in our bindings library with the root cause being a difference in how IE (including Edge) handles updates to `<textarea>` text content when setting `textarea.value`. While this does not appear to be specified in the HTML spec, Chrome, Safari, and Firefox all behave differently. Would be nice to have this specified so that libraries don't have to work around the inconsistency.

Test case:

```
var textarea = document.createElement('textarea');
var textNode = document.createTextNode('Hi');
textarea.appendChild(textNode);
console.assert(textarea.firstChild === textNode, 'Appended text node is first child of textarea.');
textarea.value = 'Yo';
console.assert(textarea.firstChild === textNode, 'After setting value, original text node remains first child of textarea.');
```

The above test passes in Chrome, Safari, and Firefox. In IE 11 Windows 7 Edge mode (and probably other IE versions), the TextNode previously a child of the textarea is replaced with a _new_ TextNode instance. This new node has the data (`Yo`) and the previous text node's data is set to empty string (`textNode.data === ''`).

In contrast, Chrome does not modify the children of the textarea when setting its value. The TextNode data is not updated at all (`textNode.data === 'Hi'`). Similarly, a user typing in a textarea does not update the TextNode data and only updates the textarea value.

As far as I can find, these behaviors are currently unspecified.

I would propose that a consistent relationship between editing the value of a textarea and its contained TextNode(s) be specified in 4.10.11 to eliminate these inconsistencies.
